e syrian  people experienced as a result of the  conflict so i want to take us back to  when  chemical weapons were used in syria and  the so-called red line  that then u.s president barack obama  sort of laid out and how it wasn't held  okay the line was not held it wasn't  that long after that  that you saw uh russia not only up its  game but also invade and occupy and  annex crimea  and how we got here partly at least  is one  we  systematically dehumanized all of these  populations by not considering them  worthy of solidarity but this isn't  a place with all due respect um  you know like iraq or afghanistan this  is a relatively civilized uh relatively  european i have to choose those words  carefully too uh city where you and we  see that very clearly in how we treat a  population that we actually do  want to engage in solidarity with so you  have all of these countries in the  european union but also the uk that are  now changing their visa requirements  tremendously and opening up their  borders in ways they never ever would  have done for afghans for syrians for  iraqis and so on right so that's what's  possible it's just that it's not  possible if you're not a ukrainian or  you're not perceived within this  quote-unquote white category so that's  one two  uh i think it sends a massive message  and did send a massive message to the  likes of putin  that  the west simply isn't going to  hold them accountable nor is the  international community more generally  so  these two things i think really go hand  in hand we're not simply  engaging in the systematic  dehumanization  of these populations we're also  encouraging  very aggressive  powers to engage in really negative ways  that impact on our security more  generally  and also promote  um a  far more disorderly uh world today we're  going to leave it there h.a hellyer  thanks for speaking with us here at the  listening post today thank you my  pleasure  and finally a story as big as the war in  ukraine the 